{
  "decision": "ROLLBACK",
  "why": "Facts from the raw log: (1) there are no errors at all - every one of the 2384 canary and 7404 stable requests returned HTTP 200; (2) canary /search carries a discrete slow tail that stable does not have: 18 of 729 canary /search requests (2.47%) land between 633 ms and 656 ms, while 0 of 2273 stable /search requests exceed 400 ms, and that tail minimum is more than 3x the stable /search p95 of 193 ms; (3) with that tail excluded, canary /search p95 is 190 ms vs stable 193 ms, so this is a tail defect and not a general slowdown; (4) the slow canary /search requests appear in all four 30 s windows (7, 2, 5, 4), so it is not a warmup or one-off blip; (5) no canary request outside /search exceeded 400 ms, so /browse and /checkout look clean. Judgement: the dashboard p95 hides this because only ~2.5% of searches are affected, which is below the 95th percentile, but the tail exists exclusively on version 1.1 and persisted steadily for the whole 120 s test, which is the signature of a real version-specific defect (e.g. a cache miss, retry or cold code path) rather than noise. At 25% traffic it already degrades ~1 in 40 searches to ~0.65 s; promoting to 100% would apply that to all search traffic with an unexplained root cause. Judgement: an unexplained 3x tail regression is cheaper to fix before release than to ship, so roll back, reproduce the 633-656 ms /search path, and re-canary after the fix.",
